#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The gate a change to how anything is drawn passes before it is called done. Usage:

    tools/gate [--quick] [--full] [--only layers,tests,audit,pool,sun,frames] [--update]
               [--machine NAME] [--keep DIR] [--fresh] [--all]
    tools/gate --seen [REV | REV..REV] [--machine NAME]

Runs, in turn, each through the tools that already hold the machine's rules (tools/wrela,
tools/audit, tools/bench, tools/gpu: one GPU job at a time, every dispatch under its line):

  layers   tools/layers: the layers' rules.
  tests    `wrela test` of lib/engine (and of lib/kit and game/world unless --quick).
  audit    The hole audit: small (160x90, 12 eyes, every kind) always; with --full the full one
           too (640x360, 24 eyes). A kind may fail only if the baseline names it as failing
           already (a debt, written down with what it is), and then no worse than it did.
  pool     The pool's judge (POOLJUDGE, `engine::judge::pool`) at `POOL_SIZE`: each pooled kind
           of the arena and the study drawn along a run and a turn at the game's fastest, its
           pooled mesh then held to a cold extraction of its cut, bit for bit (triangles, exact
           cells, outline records). It passes only when every kind is held.
  sun      The sun's judge (SUNJUDGE) on each of `SUN_VIEWS`, judged at 320x180 (drawn at
           640x360, a judged pixel's light the mean of its footprint's four): hard leaks, hard
           acne, the mean and 99th percentile error against 64 rays a pixel to the sun's disc,
           each the mean over `SUN_RUNS`: eight placements of the world's lattice of cells
           (`PLACE`) and cells a tenth narrower and wider (`CELL_PX`); and how far the mean
           error moves among them (the most less the least), which a judge of the light, not of
           where the cells fall, keeps small.
  frames   A frame's cost at 1920x1080 on each of `FRAME_SCENES`: tools/bench's span (the GPU's
           clock from a frame's first pass to its last, passes overlapped, frames back to back:
           the one measure of a frame used anywhere), the median of frames 30 to 89, the mean
           over `FRAME_PLACES` placements. And with the eye moving, on each of `MOVING_VIEWS`
           (a scene's `VIEW`: the arena's walk, the study's tour, and each eye running and
           turning at the game's fastest), over frames 30 to 269: the
           median span and the span 19 frames in 20 are within (what the frames that mesh again
           for the eye cost), each the mean over the placements.

The placements. Where the cells fall moves these numbers past their slack on its own: with the
cells 1% larger, SCENE 13 VIEW 0's hard leaks went from 754 to 882 and SCENE 13's span from 18.20
to 19.30 ms, and the lattice moved by a few mm, 677 to 816 and 19.2 to 20.5 ms. One placement is
one draw of that, so a change to the mesh would pass or fail by where it put the cells; each is
held as its mean over several placements (`engine::mesh::grid::placement`), the spread noted.

Each number is held to this machine's line in tools/gate.json (`--machine`: `m4` on macOS,
`radeon` on Linux, unless given): a count may not grow by more than `COUNT_SLACK`, an error or
a span by more than `REL_SLACK` and `ABS_SLACK`. A number that got better is said so; --update
writes what was measured as the machine's new baseline, to be committed with the change that
moved it and named in its message. A run with --only or --quick checks what it ran and leaves
the rest of the baseline as it was.

The builds. Every build the steps need (one a sun view, one a timed scene, one an audit) is made
before any step runs: the first alone (it proves the content's contracts and fills the machine's
cache of constants), the rest `WRELA_GATE_JOBS` at a time (3 unless set; a build is one thread
and under 1 GB). No build runs while the GPU is timed.

The times. The gate says how long the builds and each step took, and the whole.

What was seen. A run's result is kept on this machine by what was measured, not by when: a key
of every file under lib/, game/, toolchain/ and tools/ (tools/gate.json aside), so a commit
whose files are those has that result whether the gate ran before or after it was committed.
`--seen REV` prints what this machine's gate saw of a commit (HEAD unless given), and
`--seen A..B` a line a commit in that range: look there before a failure is bisected by running.
Results are under `~/.wrela/gate/<machine>/` (`WRELA_GATE_SEEN` names another directory). A run
whose files changed while it ran is not kept.

A RUN IS KEPT BY WHAT IT RAN. Each of the gate's runs (an audit, the pool's judge, a sun judging,
a bench, a package's tests) is a tool run on a build: what it printed is kept under
`~/.wrela/gate-runs/<machine>/`, named by the build's bytes (its manifest, shaders and program), the
tools' and the host's own bytes, and the run's arguments, and a later gate whose build and tools
are the same takes it without running anything (its step says `kept`). So a change costs the gate
only the rows it changes: none for a change to a document or a baseline, the rows of one scene
for a change to it. Only runs that ended well are kept, so a failure is run again. `--fresh`
keeps nothing and takes nothing kept. The tests run beside the GPU's steps (they use no GPU), and
the gate stops at the first step that fails: `--all` runs every step whatever fails.

Exit status: 0 when everything run held its line; 1 when something didn't; 2 for a usage error.
`--seen REV`: 0 when the commit was seen to pass, 1 seen to fail, 3 not seen.
"""

